          body:
            "Beginners often imagine that a computer \"understands\" what they are doing: they type in a box, the website \"knows\" their name, the app \"remembers\" their position. None of that is true, and understanding why it is not true is the whole point of this lesson.\n\nA computer does not understand anything. It is a machine with a very small vocabulary of operations it can perform — adding, comparing, moving bits around — and it performs them extremely quickly, in a fixed order, without understanding a single word of the instructions. Everything that looks like understanding is built out of that machinery. An app that \"remembers\" your name is storing a pattern of bits on a disk or in a memory chip; the app that \"knows\" where you are is comparing numbers.\n\nSo the two questions this lesson answers are: **what is the small vocabulary a computer actually has?**, and **how do you build the appearance of understanding out of it?** The answer to the first is a four-stage loop — input, store, process, output — and the answer to the second is abstraction: layers of software that hide what is underneath while relying on it.",
