          kind: "definition",
          body:
            "**Hardware** is the physical machine: the processor (CPU) that executes instructions, the memory (RAM) that holds data while the machine is running, the storage (SSD, flash) that holds data while it is off, and the devices — screen, keyboard, network card, microphone — through which data enters and leaves. None of it stores \"programs\" in any special way; a program on disk is just another pattern of bits, no different in kind from a photograph.\n\n**Software** is any set of instructions the hardware executes. A **program** is one set of instructions written by a person (or generated by one). An **algorithm** is a procedure — a finite sequence of unambiguous steps — that solves a class of problems; a program is an algorithm written in a language a machine can run.\n\n**The input–store–process–output loop** is the model this lesson teaches:\n\n- **Input** — data arrives from the world: keystrokes, a tap, a sensor, a file, a request from another machine.\n- **Store** — data is kept somewhere — memory while running, storage when off — so it can be used later rather than now.\n- **Process** — data is transformed: sorted, displayed, checked, combined with other data.\n- **Output** — the result goes back to the world: pixels on a screen, sound from a speaker, a file written, a message sent.\n\nThe loop is not a diagram of one program; it is what *every* program does, repeatedly, for as long as it runs. A spell-checker takes a word, looks it up in a stored dictionary, compares it, and puts a squiggle on screen. A video player takes encoded frames from the network, decodes them, and writes them to the screen sixty times a second. A bank's system takes a transfer request, reads two balances, subtracts from one and adds to the other, and writes both back.\n\n**Abstraction** is the tool that lets humans build on top of this loop without being overwhelmed by it. Each layer of a computer system hides the layers below and offers a simpler promise: an application does not know about electrons, a web page does not know about radio waves, a function does not know about the loop that called it. Every layer is real, and every layer relies on the layer below keeping its promise.",
