            "The natural objection is: *surely some programs do more than this?* They do not — but the loop runs at different speeds and at different scales, and that is where the appearance of complexity comes from.\n\n**Speed.** A modern processor executes billions of instructions per second. When the loop repeats two billion times a second, the input can be a camera's video frames, the processing can be real-time image filters, and the output can be a video call. Nothing was added to the vocabulary; only the rate changed.\n\n**Scale.** When millions of loops run at once across thousands of machines and exchange *each other's output* as input, you get a search engine, a social network, a mobile-money system. Nothing was added to the vocabulary either; the outputs of one loop became the inputs of another. This is the essential insight of **distributed systems**, and it is why the network lesson later in this course matters so much.\n\n**Composition.** A program's output can be another program's input — a compiler takes source code and produces an executable; a template engine takes a template and produces HTML; a build tool takes your files and produces a bundle. The loop is fractal: the same four stages appear at every scale, from one keystroke to one data centre.\n\nThis also explains why a computer can do anything *specified* and nothing *unspecified*. A computer has no idea what you meant. If the algorithm does not say what to do when the list is empty, the program will not do the sensible thing; it will do whatever the unspecified case happens to produce — a crash, a wrong number, or a `NaN` that appears three screens later. \"The computer did something stupid\" almost always means \"the instructions were incomplete\", and this lesson's loop is how you start to see where.",
