            "Scale the same four stages up from one keystroke to an entire product — a learning platform like this one — and notice that nothing new is introduced, only more data and more repetition.\n\n**Input, at four boundaries.** A student types an email and password into a form; the browser collects those values and sends them as an HTTP request. A teacher uploads a video file from their laptop; the browser streams it in chunks. A payment provider sends a webhook saying money moved. A scheduler fires every minute asking \"are any sessions about to expire?\". Four different kinds of input, four different arrival patterns, one loop each.\n\n**Store, at three levels.** The request handler holds the decoded request in memory for the milliseconds it needs — stack and heap of that one process. The database holds the durable truth: accounts, lessons, progress rows, invoices — on disk, with a cache of hot pages in RAM so reads do not reach the disk constantly. A content delivery network holds copies of the lesson videos near the students, so the bytes travel once from the origin and then sit close to the people watching. Each level exists for a reason: milliseconds for the request, years for the database, continents for the cache.\n\n**Process, at five kinds of work.** Verification: hash the supplied password and compare it with the stored hash — never compare the password itself. Business rules: does this student own the program this lesson belongs to? Transform: compute a course percentage from completed lesson ids. Aggregate: sum minutes learned across every student for the teacher's dashboard. Serialise: turn rows into JSON (or HTML) for the response. Every one of these is arithmetic and branching — the processor's entire vocabulary — applied to data that arrived as input.\n\n**Output, at four exits.** HTML or JSON back to the browser that asked; a file streamed to a student's video player; an email handed to a delivery service; a receipt sent to the payment provider. Each is the loop's output becoming someone else's input — the browser's next request, the student's eyes, the provider's own system.\n\nThen the loop repeats: a thousand students produce a thousand concurrent loops, each cycle taking tens of milliseconds, all of them sharing the same storage. When the site \"feels slow\", you now have a precise vocabulary for asking why — was the input slow to arrive (network), was the store slow to answer (disk or lock contention), was the process slow (an expensive query, an unindexed lookup), or was the output large (an oversized response, an unoptimised image)? Those four questions, applied in order, diagnose the overwhelming majority of performance problems, and they are nothing more than the loop turned into a debugging method.",
