          heading: "Common mistakes and misconceptions",
          kind: "warning",
          body:
            "- **\"The computer remembers.\"** Storage is a pattern of bits somewhere. Nothing is remembered; something is *written*, and it can be lost, corrupted, overwritten or leaked. Every data feature you build is a storage feature you must also make safe.\n- **\"The app knows.\"** A program compares and counts. \"Knowing\" is what the comparison looks like from outside. This matters practically: when an app \"knows\" something about you, a row in a database says it, and rows can be queried, exported and subpoenaed.\n- **\"Code runs.\"** Code does not run; a *runtime* interprets or executes it, and the runtime is another program with its own rules. \"It works on my machine\" is the sound of two runtimes disagreeing.\n- **Confusing the algorithm with the program.** The algorithm is the idea — sorting by repeatedly taking the smallest remaining item. The program is one expression of it, in one language, with one set of bugs.\n- **Thinking input and output are only keyboard and screen.** Network requests are input, files are input and output, the clock is input, a random number generator is input, sensors are input. Broaden the definition early.\n- **\"Storage is memory.\"** RAM is volatile and fast; storage is persistent and slow — by roughly five orders of magnitude. Which one you use is a design decision with performance consequences.\n- **Believing hardware is the hard part.** The hardware is astonishing and completely commoditised; almost all the difficulty in software lives in specifying the process stage correctly.\n- **Skipping the store stage.** Beginners jump straight from input to output and hardcode state, which is why their programs cannot remember anything and cannot handle two users at once.",
